### Block 3 - Dirty checking, flush and transactions
### Select the "local" environment. Needs at least one product (see 01-entities.http).

### PATCH a product. Look at ProductService.update: there is no save() call.
### Q: why is there an UPDATE if we never call save()?
### Q: what would happen without @Transactional?
PATCH {{host}}/products/{{productId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "stock": 25
}

### Partial update: only the name (stock stays untouched)
PATCH {{host}}/products/{{productId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Mechanical keyboard"
}

### PATCH a product that does not exist
PATCH {{host}}/products/99999
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "stock": 1
}

### Dirty checking: a change produces 1 UPDATE, the same value again produces 0
### Q: what does Hibernate compare to decide the entity is dirty?
POST {{host}}/demo/dirty-checking?productId={{productId}}&stock=50

### Flush step by step (rollback=true: executed but never committed)
### Q: setStock was called 3 times, how many UPDATEs before the first flush?
### Q: why did the JPQL query flush before running?
### Q: what is the stock in the DB after a rollback?
POST {{host}}/demo/flush?productId={{productId}}&rollback=true

### Same flow but committed
### Q: save() vs flush() vs commit - which one makes the change permanent?
POST {{host}}/demo/flush?productId={{productId}}&rollback=false
